Z||000000||Brandon86SE||09-24-2001||02:43 AM||||Me and a friend are doing some work to my car tomorrow.  We're changing my alternator/ water pump belt, and installing a 3-way gauge pod (ammeter, water temp., oil pressure) where the factory oil pressure/ voltmeter gauges are in the center console.  My friend knows a lot about cars, and has done numerous belts, but we were looking at it the other day, and kinda wondering the best way to get at it.  So basically, I'm just checking to see if anyone knows something I should be watching out for when doing this work, and also hoping someone can tell me the best way of getting at the belt. Right now, we're thinking of taking out the battery......<P>Thanks in advance!<P>Brandon<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Field_Front_(sig)_1.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||varnster||09-24-2001||09:10 PM||||The reason your not getting any response is because their is alot of info on this subject<BR>in the archives, just take care applying force to the alt mount it is made of alluminum and will brake, also take care with the top pivet bolt you can strip it out if you apply to much torque,also put some <BR>antiseize on these bolts for the future.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||SLYGUY||09-25-2001||04:30 AM||||I changed the alternator on my GT and the easiest way that I can think of is to jack the car up. Use BOTH a pair of jackstands AND a floor jack that is in good condition.<BR>I had the left rear tire on a ramp, blocked the front tires, and had the right rear in the air with a jack and jackstands. <P>Take there right rear tire off. Makes it easier and you can see much more. Be careful, as mentioned in an earlier post, with the alternator mount bolt. You would really hate to break that off.<P>Sly||||reg||1
